PostgreSQL has one concept — the role — that plays the part of both user and group. LOGIN is just an attribute.
-- Group roles carry the privileges; login roles inherit them.
CREATE ROLE app_read NOLOGIN;
CREATE ROLE app_write NOLOGIN;
CREATE ROLE app_svc LOGIN PASSWORD '...' IN ROLE app_write;
GRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE shop TO app_read;
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO app_read;
GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO app_read;
GRANT INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO app_write;
GRANT app_read TO app_write;
-- ALL TABLES applies only to tables that exist NOW. For future ones:
ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA public
GRANT SELECT ON TABLES TO app_read;
ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA public
GRANT USAGE, SELECT ON SEQUENCES TO app_write;
ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES applies to objects created by the role that runs it. Run it as the role that owns your migrations, or specify FOR ROLE migrator, or the next table created will silently be inaccessible.
Baseline hardening:
The application never connects as a superuser, and never as the schema owner. A compromised application should not be able to DROP TABLE.
Revoke the default public access: since PostgreSQL 15 the public schema no longer grants CREATE to PUBLIC, but on older clusters run REVOKE CREATE ON SCHEMA public FROM PUBLIC;.
REVOKE ALL ON DATABASE shop FROM PUBLIC; then grant CONNECT explicitly.
Separate roles for migrations (DDL), the application (DML), read replicas/analytics (SELECT only), and backups (pg_read_all_data, a predefined role since PostgreSQL 14).

Create a two-tenant documents table with RLS. Show that a query with app.tenant_id = '1' cannot see tenant 2's rows, that FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY also constrains the owner, and that omitting WITH CHECK lets a tenant insert a row it then cannot read back.
Solution
ALTER TABLE documents ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE documents FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
CREATE POLICY tenant_all ON documents
FOR ALL
USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id')::bigint)
WITH CHECK (tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id')::bigint);
BEGIN; SET LOCAL app.tenant_id = '1';
SELECT count(*) FROM documents; -- tenant 1 only
INSERT INTO documents(tenant_id, owner, body) VALUES (2, 'x', 'y');
-- ERROR: new row violates row-level security policy for table "documents"
COMMIT;
CREATE INDEX ON documents (tenant_id, id); -- the policy predicate needs an index
